WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.060
- [Jared] I started making
these maps to have a

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:05.500
beautiful representation of
the places I've traveled:

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.770
Yosemite, Zion, Yellowstone,

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.573
Rocky Mountain National
Park and many more.

00:00:10.573 --> 00:00:11.406
(water splashes)

00:00:11.406 --> 00:00:13.989
(upbeat music)

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:20.500
What makes Frank Church Wilderness

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.993
unique is just how large it is.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.720
You could fit all of those national parks

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.430
within the boundaries of the Frank Church.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:29.350
- My name is Jared Prince.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:31.130
I'm the founder of Muir Way

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.350
and a designer of artistic maps.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.500
I started making these
maps to have a beautiful

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.890
representation of the
places I've traveled.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:41.060
My maps aren't navigational maps.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.535
They're meant to be hung on your wall,

00:00:42.535 --> 00:00:45.053
so that they can inspire on a daily basis.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.860
- The Frank Church is part of the larger

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:52.010
Rocky Mountain range and has
no entry point for humanity.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.520
It has no roads. The
only way to get in is by

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:58.890
airplane or by foot, by
horseback or you have to boat in.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:00:59.863
- Usually, when I make a map,

00:00:59.863 --> 00:01:02.090
it's of a place that I've already been.

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:04.570
Coors Light has brought
me to the Frank Church,

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:06.860
I'm getting to fly above
the wilderness and just

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.730
see all the beautiful
mountains and vistas.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:11.460
It puts everything in perspective

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.450
when you see it from a distance.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.470
- Looking at the map,
now this is so cool to

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:18.410
see it stripped down
and just seeing the raw

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:21.010
contour lines, the topography like that.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.860
This is one of the few
wild places left in America

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.655
and it needed to be preserved.

00:01:25.655 --> 00:01:27.786
Ah, God, I love this place.

00:01:28.936 --> 00:01:31.603
(Country Music)

00:01:39.158 --> 00:01:41.680
(plane engine buzzes)

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.013
We are now officially at the wilderness.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.860
- [Jared] You can see how
special this place is to Mike.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.643
- This view looking down
the middle fork is one of--

00:01:52.643 --> 00:01:53.940
- [Jared] You know it's just as

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.219
special to countless other people.

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:59.930
I mean, on the map you
see the contour lines,

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:03.140
so close together, you know
that it's a steep incline.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:04.910
But then when you see it in real

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:08.200
life, it's just otherworldly.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.070
- [Mike] No matter what direction I look,

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:15.640
it's all completely road-less wilderness.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.250
- [Jared] The geographic
features are the highways.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.003
Instead of a highway, you've got a river.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.440
Instead of street names
you have mountain peaks,

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.933
you have valleys, you have creeks.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:30.637
This is how you get around.

00:02:34.461 --> 00:02:35.686
(river babbles)

00:02:35.686 --> 00:02:36.560
- [Man] Everything's OK?

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.343
- [Jared] Yeah.

00:02:38.343 --> 00:02:41.093
(water splashes)

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.720
- [Jared] I want to see
how accurate and detailed

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.750
this map that I'm creating is.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.440
I want to get the colors
of the place, rivers,

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:55.323
the bends, mountains, the
ruggedness of the wilderness.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.860
That's the essence of a map, is that

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.003
it inspires exploration and wonder.

00:03:03.113 --> 00:03:04.550
(bee buzzes)

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.140
- [Jared] For this map we're going to

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.580
donate a portion of the
sales of every map to the

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.290
Leave No Trace organization,
which promotes the education

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:17.290
and values of being a
responsible camper in nature.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.830
We can all make a difference
to keep places like this

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:23.593
beautiful, serene and clean
for future generations.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:28.640
When I look at this Frank
Church map, I'll remember

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.460
the river I rafted down
and the piece that I saw in

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:34.663
the airplane, which was
a magical experience.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:37.730
My hope is that you get
a map for an adventure

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:39.430
that you had and that you get a map

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:41.453
for an adventure that you want to have.

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:45.900
I'm very proud of what I create.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.330
These maps should last a lifetime,

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:51.830
just like memories that were formed there.

